 A fresh, properly bounded candidate that passes complete calibration and untouched validation, with independently checked references and the required tuning authority, would overturn the present unready verdict for that scope. Posterior/default promotion would still require the later evidence gates. The weakest evidence here is the limited two-dataset nomination screen and the absence of prior-wide exclusion and posterior comparisons; no conclusion about those quantities is drawn from a finite score or healthy short HMC run.
 
 This closeout is the executor's source/evidence review. No independent terminal Claude review was performed. The existing material plan review remains available, but it is not being represented as a review of these implementation results. The next substantive experiment requires a revised LG1 repair program because the authorized attempt allowance is exhausted.
+
+## Follow-up: apparent LGSSM regression
+
+The subsequent [fixed-input regression audit](ledh-lg1-regression-audit-result-2026-09-18.md) reproduced one failed LG1 case on the starting and revised implementations. Both have approximately 16.0006% maximum covariance error; their log likelihoods differ by only 3.8e-6. The actual consumer agrees. Thus the recent containment changes did not cause that replayed accuracy failure. This does not rule out regressions in other configurations or the N4096 multi-block path.
+
+The specific September 3 LGSSM tuning SUCCESS report used a different Cubature/GenUT evaluator and a numerical-validity screen without Kalman accuracy. It is not a matched canonical-LEDH accuracy baseline. Other prior LGSSM successes were not exhaustively reviewed. The original campaign should have established this distinction before explaining the apparent regression; its candidate rejection remains valid, while a broader claim that earlier LGSSM success was lost is unsupported. The follow-up used no new calibration candidate and did not reopen the original attempt allowance.
